  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn as a surface at 0.5 × loss. It is 3 times steeper left-right, so too large a rate overshoots there first. Steps diverge when the rate is above 2/3, as at 1.",
    "Step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is random, centred on zero, with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Arrows are horizontal and drawn at true step length (1 scene unit per unit). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes; grey is −rate × true slope. A hidden arrow means a step length of 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or when the loss passes 6, the bowl's edge in the steep direction (diverged). Past the edge the ball is held at the bowl's rim.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence. Each new run uses the next noise values. The ghost keeps the last finished path (up to 40 steps). One step every 0.2 s at 1× speed."
  ]
}